    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How to Refinance When Your Financial Situation Has Changed Since Your Original Mortgage</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you bought your home when you had a W-2 job, a different credit profile, or more equity than you currently hold, and you want to refinance now that circumstances have shifted, the path forward is more nuanced than a standard refinance application. The options available to you depend on which aspect of your profile has changed and by how much, and understanding that before you apply determines whether you find the right product or end up in a process that was never going to work.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Three Changes That Complicate a Refinance Most</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Income changes, credit changes, and equity changes each create different complications in a refinance application, and each has different solutions.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">An income change, whether from a job loss, a career transition, self-employment, or a reduction in hours, affects how a lender calculates your debt-to-income ratio. A borrower who qualified under W-2 income and is now self-employed faces the same documentation challenges in a refinance that a self-employed purchase borrower faces. Two years of self-employment history, tax returns showing adequate net income, and bank statements that support the qualification are all required under conventional guidelines.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A credit change that has moved your score lower since your original mortgage affects the rate you'll qualify for and, in some cases, whether you qualify at all under certain programs. A score that has dropped significantly from when you first closed can mean the rate improvement from a refinance is smaller than expected, or that a Non-QM product is now required to access financing at all.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">An equity change matters most when your home has declined in value or you've taken on additional liens since purchase. A standard refinance typically requires at least some equity cushion. Homeowners who are at or near the balance of their home's current value have fewer conventional options and need to explore government programs specifically designed for low-equity refinances.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Option: Programs Designed for Difficult Refinance Scenarios</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's what most homeowners in a changed financial situation don't know. Several programs exist specifically for borrowers who don't fit the standard refinance profile.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For homeowners with limited equity, the FHA streamline refinance allows refinancing without a new appraisal, which removes the current value of the home as a barrier for existing FHA borrowers. The VA IRRRL works similarly for eligible veterans.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For homeowners who are now self-employed or whose income documentation has changed, Non-QM refinance products apply the same alternative documentation approaches available on the purchase side. Bank statement refinances, profit and loss refinances, and DSCR refinances for investment properties all exist and function similarly to their purchase counterparts.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For homeowners whose credit has declined but who have significant equity, some lenders offer portfolio loan refinances with more flexible credit guidelines in exchange for a lower LTV and higher rate.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Identify specifically which aspect of your financial profile has changed and by how much.</strong> Income, credit, and equity each point toward different products. Knowing which one is the constraint tells you which solution to look for.</li>
      <li><strong>Pull your current credit report and calculate your current DTI before you apply.</strong> Understanding your actual current position rather than your assumed position is the starting point for finding a product that fits.</li>
      <li><strong>Get a current market value estimate on your home before you evaluate refinance options.</strong> If your equity position is tighter than when you first bought, knowing your actual LTV determines which programs are available.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask lenders specifically about Non-QM refinance options if your income documentation has changed.</strong> A lender who only does conventional refinances will tell you that you don't qualify. A lender with a broader product set may have a path that works.</li>
      <li><strong>If you're underwater or near it, ask your current servicer about their modification or refinance options before you approach a new lender.</strong> Some servicers have internal programs for existing customers in difficult equity positions that aren't available through third-party lenders.</li>
    </ol>

